<!--
    kuna GH-878: a pointer gcc keeps in $v1 across a call to a callee that
    never writes $v1 printed as a local nothing assigns (or, in decompile-all,
    as the high word of the call's result), and the function lost the
    parameter it came from.

    Stage / sub-stage: P3 call guard (Heritage::guardCalls), the callee-body
    write probe (p4_calls/kuna_rustabi.rs probe_callee_return_writes) and its
    consumers p4_calls/kuna_calleeretpreserves.rs and kuna_calleepreserves.rs.
    P0 assertions: option calleeretpreserves and option calleepreserves
    (both on is the shipped default).

    Two defects. The probe never finished a MIPS or ARM body: every `jr ra`
    and `bx lr` runs the setISAMode user op, which stopped the walk, so no
    body there proved a register untouched. It now reads through that user op.
    And a callee that writes any return register ($v0) gave no answer for the
    other ($v1); it now answers per register, for a return register no
    argument travels in.

    Source (mipsel-linux-gnu-gcc 10.3 -O2, linked -nostdlib -static):
        long G[4];
        int hk(int x)    { return (int)G[2] + x; }      lui v0; lw v0; jr ra; addu v0,a0,v0
        int pick(int x)  { if (x > 3) return (int)G[2] + x; return (int)G[1] - x; }
                                                         two returns, both write only v0
        void setg(int x) { G[1] = x; }                  lui v0; jr ra; sw a0,(v0)
        long long wide(int x) { return (long long)G[3] * x; }   mult; mflo v0; jr ra; mfhi v1
        int afterstore2(long *p) { int r = hk(5);   G[0] = p[0]; return r; }
        int afterbranch(long *p) { int r = pick(5); G[0] = p[0]; return r; }
        int aftervoid(long *p, int q) { setg(q); G[0] = p[0]; return q; }
        int hiword(void) { return (int)(wide(3) >> 32); }
    Each after* caller is `move v1,a0; jal f; ...; lw a0,0(v1)`: gcc -fipa-ra
    knows f never writes $v1 and keeps p there across the call.

    Pass 1 (both options off) pins the bug: $v1 is an unassigned local and the
    parameter is gone. Pass 2 (on) reads p through a0. aftervoid is kept by
    calleepreserves (setg stores and writes only $v0), the other two by
    calleeretpreserves. hiword is the control: wide writes $v1, so the read of
    $v1 stays the high word of its result in both passes. Pass 3 declares
    hk as returning int, as DWARF does: the declaration leaves $v1 out of the
    call's output, and afterstore2 still reads p.
-->
